  "id": "v04",
  "title": "Idioms & Phrases",
  "intro": "Idioms are fixed figurative expressions whose metaphorical meaning cannot be derived from the literal definitions of the individual words. WBPSC Clerkship tests common idioms rooted in colors, human anatomy, animal traits, and classic maritime expressions.",
  "bengali": "Idioms & Phrases (বাগধারা) হলো এমন বিশিষ্টার্থক শব্দগুচ্ছ যার আক্ষরিক অর্থ ও ভাবার্থ সম্পূর্ণ আলাদা। যেমন: 'A bolt from the blue' আক্ষরিক অর্থে নীল আকাশ থেকে বজ্রপাত নয়, এর ভাবার্থ হলো 'বিনা মেঘে বজ্রপাত' বা আকস্মিক অপ্রত্যাশিত বিপদ। মুখস্থ করার কৌশল হলো প্রতিটি বাগধারার পেছনের মূল ভাব বা উৎস বোঝা। অপশনে যদি আক্ষরিক অর্থ (literal meaning) দেওয়া থাকে, তবে ৯৯% ক্ষেত্রে সেটি ভুল উত্তর।",
  "rules": [
    {
      "rule": "The Literal Trap Rule: Never select an option that interprets the words of an idiom literally; idiomatic meaning is always metaphorical and figurative.",
      "examples": [
        "'Bite the bullet' does NOT mean biting ammunition; it means facing a grim situation courageously.",
        "'Spill the beans' does NOT mean dropping vegetables; it means disclosing a confidential secret prematurely."
      ],
      "exceptions": [
        "On rare occasions, the origin of an idiom is factual, but its application remains figurative."
      ]
    },
    {
      "rule": "Color-based Idioms and their conventional metaphorical associations in English.",
      "examples": [
        "'Once in a blue moon': Extremely rarely.",
        "'Out of the blue': Unexpectedly, without warning.",
        "'See red': Become violently angry.",
        "'In the red': Operating at a financial loss.",
        "'Red-letter day': A memorable, auspicious, or joyful day.",
        "'White elephant': A burdensome, costly possession with no practical utility."
      ],
      "exceptions": [
        "'White lie' means a harmless lie told to avoid hurting someone's feelings."
      ]
    },
    {
      "rule": "Anatomy / Body-part Idioms reflecting human emotions and behaviors.",
      "examples": [
        "'At arm's length': Keeping at a safe distance; avoiding excessive intimacy.",
        "'Turn a deaf ear to': Refuse deliberately to listen or heed advice.",
        "'See eye to eye': Agree entirely on an issue.",
        "'Bone of contention': The root subject of a dispute.",
        "'By the skin of one's teeth': Barely escaping disaster; by a narrow margin."
      ],
      "exceptions": [
        "'Cry over spilt milk' means futile regret over past irrecoverable loss."
      ]
    },
    {
      "rule": "Animal and Nature Metaphors common in exam papers.",
      "examples": [
        "'Crocodile tears': Feigned, insincere sorrow.",
        "'A dark horse': An unexpected winner whose abilities were unrevealed.",
        "'Wild goose chase': A futile, foolish, and hopeless search.",
        "'Smell a rat': Suspect foul play or deceit.",
        "'At a snail's pace': Moving exceedingly slowly."
      ],
      "exceptions": [
        "'Lion's share' means the largest and major portion of something."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "Picking an option containing the literal objects mentioned in the idiom (e.g. selecting 'elephants' for 'white elephant').",
    "Confusing 'Once in a blue moon' (rare) with 'Out of the blue' (sudden).",
    "Confusing 'Break the ice' (initiate conversation) with 'Break a leg' (good luck).",
    "Confusing 'Fair-weather friend' (a friend only in good times) with a true friend.",
    "Misinterpreting 'Achilles' heel' as a strength instead of a vulnerable weak spot."
